  <participating-org role="4" type="24">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The She Craft Women Group was founded in 2017 and officially registered in January 2019 as a CBO, the group was founded by women who returned from the LRA captivity and once participated as fighters, and others are mothers to children born in captivity and singlehandedly trekking places in search for meaningful livelihoods and options for survival.

While in the rehabilitation centers, TrustFund for victim through Gulu Women Economic Development and Globalization [GWED-G] trained them on specialized skills for craft making and tailoring to support their livelihoods as they integrate back in the community. The women business progressed as they operated within their homes and only merged when they had big orders to supply. The aim of building capacities of the She Craft Women Groups is to provide mentorship, coaching and create a platform for their self-empowerment for them to influence decision making processes for women survivors of war and sexual violence.

Some society actors rendered support to our needs however they have been limited, in adequate and not victim/gender sensitive. The She Craft groups of women are 30women members with children born in captivity andstarted working as a group to increase on their agency, economic potentials and voice.

&#xa7; Increased income worth 9,000,000UGX raised through tailored made crafts and materials, which enabled them to send their children to school and eat equal meal.
&#xa7; Group members gained more opportunities to work with local partners to develop their own entrepreneurial skills and attitude that respond to real life challenges.
&#xa7; Benefited from linkages to various local business incubators. Practiced good leadership, governance and accountability.
&#xa7; The women group&apos;s members gained skills for family redress, parenting, and psychosocial support to individual members, and rendering life skills education to their adolescent&apos;s girls in school.
&#xa7; Started with correct financial management practices of proper record keeping of accounts, opened account with Stanbic bank, having receipts books, while taking routine stock checking. They also attended to various financial literacy training.
&#xa7; Quality of products improved and more customers attracted hence market and visibility increased.
&#xa7; The group members have gained more confidence and increased on their advocacy skills within their own communities addressing issues of violence and discriminations in their family and communities.</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The She Craft Women&apos;s Group</narrative>
